{#
    A configuration value: the name a reader searches for, what it takes, and
    what happens if they leave it alone.

    The element draws all of it — see `card.html.twig` for why every option
    is a property and no markup of a component is written here. What used to
    stand in this file was a hand-built definition list carrying a class no
    stylesheet defined, which is how a reference ended up styled by the
    document layer's `dt` and `dd` and by nothing that knew what it was.

    The core template labels the default `Type:` — the same word it uses for
    the type, two lines above. That is an upstream defect and this is where it
    stops being one.

    **The facts go in as text**, through `plain` — see `ThemeExtension`.
    `:type:` and `:default:` are parsed inline, so they can hold a link, and an
    attribute flattens one to its words. That is the trade: a type is written
    `array<string>` as often as it is written as a reference, and markup in a
    value that reads `<string>` is markup that eats half the type. The
    description keeps every block it was written with.
#}
{%- set facts = [] -%}
{%- for key, option in node.additionalOptions -%}
    {%- set facts = facts|merge([{ label: key, value: renderNode(option)|plain }]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
<sds-confval
    name="{{ node.plainContent }}"
    {%- if node.isNoindex == false %} anchor="{{ node.anchor }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if node.required %} required{% endif %}
    {%- if node.type %} type="{{ renderNode(node.type)|plain }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if node.default != null %} default="{{ renderNode(node.default)|plain }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if facts %} facts="{{ facts|json_encode }}"{% endif -%}
>{{ renderNode(node.value) }}</sds-confval>
